      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Focus on the \"human dignity\" argument by exposing how giving money to everyone for exactly the same amount of poverty erases the distinction between human worth and mere survival rights, effectively treating citizens as cash registers rather than individuals striving upward. For the \"automation\" point, flatly reject the fatalist assumption that technology is the sole driver of unemployment, arguing that human agency, which is the Pro side's weakest link, ignores history where technology consistently created more jobs than it destroyed while simultaneously fueling the prosperity of those displaced by capital. Instead of accepting their doom-saying narrative, force your student to demand specific projections on job creation rates or uncover the logical fallacy that everyone wanting to be a coder automatically qualifies the entire workforce, leaving them to argue that an idealized future benefits only a privileged few while mass unemployment actually creates, not solves, the dignity problem you described in your first prompt.",
