      "content": "Do not dismiss that economic claim entirely, as modern macroeconomics does not support such blunt rejection; instead, argue that UBI would only bubble up the entire economy on the backs of a few people in cyclical economic good times and as the government hits a deficit wall if that spending had ever existed before. By focusing on the key flaw that UBI is a fiscal policy that distracts from actual structural economic policy reforms and ignores that our economy has been growing slower in long bull markets due to income inequality than it has in downturns, you can expose why this argument is just ideological cherry-picking rather than mathematical analysis. The cost of funding that mythical \"spending engine\" would likely outweigh its minor, temporary benefits, leading to a bumpy road with a large deficit and a lack of benefit for the average American who really needs aid.",
